  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 PM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A Heat Advisory has been issued for Friday 11 \nAM until 8 PM. Anomalously hot temperatures are expected to \npersist into Sat at least for a portion of central NC.\n\nA lower mid-latitude/sub-tropical ridge centered over the srn \nAppalachians, observed around 595 dam and 324 dam in the 500-700\nmb layer this morning, will persist in a favorable location for\nexcessive heat in cntl NC for the next couple of days. \n\nPersistence surface temperatures, to 1-2 F hotter ones \nespecially over the nrn Piedmont, will likely result today and \ncombine with mixed/afternoon dewpoints in the upr 60s to lwr \n70s. Related heat index values will range between 100-105 F over\nthe nw Piedmont to mostly 105-108 F in the Advisory area. Any \nconvective chances continue to decrease throughout mid-afteroon \nto provide any relief from dangerous heat.\n\nMean mixing ratios and surface dewpoints are forecast to \nincrease slightly on Fri, when mixed/afternoon dewpoints are \nlikely to be in the lwr 70s. That uptick in dewpoints will \nlikely yield the highest heat index values of this stretch on \nFri, when values near 110 F will be possible from the nrn \nSandhills to the ne Piedmont and Coastal Plain. As such, a Heat \nAdvisory has been issued for Fri (11 AM until 8 PM). The Heat \nAdvisory for today, Thurs, remains in effect until 8 PM this \nevening. Additional Heat Advisories will likely be needed, but \nperhaps over a smaller area, on Sat.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Storm chances will increase on Friday and peak\non Saturday ahead of a cold front. This will be followed by \ndrier weather and a return to more seasonal temperatures early \nnext week.\n\nMid to upper-level northwest flow will increase Friday and peak\non Saturday. The anomalous ridge will gradually be shunted \nwestward by Saturday as an East Coast trough becomes more \ndominant. This pattern shift will increasingly favor showers and\nthunderstorms through the first half of the weekend.\n\nFriday: The dangerous heat dome will remain in place. Model \nguidance indicates a morning disturbance tracking across the \nGreat Lakes and northern Mid-Atlantic, which may sustain ongoing\nearly-day convection. By the afternoon and evening, several 12z\nHREF members initiate new storms along the southern Virginia \nborder, spreading into northern portions of central and eastern \nNorth Carolina. Organization may be enhanced by an upstream \ndisturbance, a surface trough, or lingering outflow boundaries. \nThese storms could grow upscale as they track east-southeast \ninto the Piedmont and Coastal Plain through the mid-to-late \nevening. Damaging winds and large hail are the primary hazards. \nA Slight Risk for severe weather remains in place for much of \nthe northern Piedmont, the Triangle, and the central/northern \nCoastal Plain, with a Marginal Risk covering the remaining \nsouthwestern and western areas.\n\nSaturday: The greatest coverage of showers and thunderstorms is\nexpected on Saturday. A well-defined, convectively-induced mid-\nlevel disturbance over the lower Ohio Valley will interact with\na backdoor cold front sliding south through the Mid-Atlantic. \nDeep-layer shear will increase compared to Friday as the East \nCoast trough digs further west, supporting multicell storms \ncapable of producing damaging wind gusts and large hail. A \nSlight Risk remains in place for all of central North Carolina. \nAhead of this convective activity, expect another day of \ndangerous heat.\n\nSunday & Beyond: The cold front is expected to push through by \nSunday morning. While post-frontal stratus, lingering moisture, \nand upslope flow could support isolated to scattered showers \nacross the western Piedmont, the severe threat will have greatly\ndiminished. Drier air and more seasonal temperatures will \narrive in time for Labor Day and persist into Tuesday. \nUnfortunately, this reprieve from the heat will be brief, with \nhigh temperatures expected to rebound into the low-to-mid 90s by\nWednesday and Thursday. While some ensemble guidance hints at \nanother frontal passage and a renewed chance for precipitation \nlate next week, confidence remains low at this range.",
